    "bounding box" : [ 105.12, 745.329, 236.518, 756.444 ],
    "font" : "TimesNewRoman",
    "font size" : 10.036,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "IV. DIRECT MIMO QFT DESIGN"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 29,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 51.06, 680.829, 290.662, 738.744 ],
    "font" : "TimesNewRoman",
    "font size" : 10.036,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "The MIMO QFT design technique provides a design procedure to synthesize a fixed diagonal controller transfer function matrix G(s) and prefilter F(s) to satisfy specifications on the closed-loop system shown in Fig.2, where P(s) is the MIMO uncertain plant."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 30,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 51.061, 575.527, 290.636, 680.243 ],
    "font" : "TimesNewRoman",
    "font size" : 10.036,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "The basic principle for MIMO QFT design is to convert the MIMO control system into a set of equivalent MISO control systems. Using fixed-point theorem, the MIMO control problem for an m × m system can be converted into m equivalent single-loop MISO problems, each with two inputs and one output. The objective of the design is to achieve set point tracking, while minimizing the outputs due to the disturbance inputs (cross-coupling effects) [5, 6]."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 31,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 51.06, 534.129, 240.358, 568.944 ],
    "font" : "TimesNewRoman",
    "font size" : 10.036,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "V. COMBINED QFT/EEAS DESIGN A. Problem formulation"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 32,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 51.06, 444.551, 290.691, 533.543 ],
    "font" : "TimesNewRoman",
    "font size" : 10.036,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "With no loss of generality and for simplicity, the design process is developed for 2 × 2 MIMO plants. The procedure can be easily extended to the general MIMO case. Consider the feedback structure shown in Fig. 3. The transfer function matrix P(s) = [pij (s)] , i, j = 1,2 represents the LTI uncertain 2 × 2 MIMO plant to be controlled. The Gi = diag[gi1(s),gi2(s)], i =1,2,3 and"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 33,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 51.06, 402.851, 290.635, 443.589 ],
    "font" : "TimesNewRoman",
    "font size" : 10.036,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "F(s) = diag [ f11(s), f22(s)], which are assumed diagonal, represent the feedforward compensators and the prefilter matrix, respectively. Also, the nonlinear elements N1and"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 34,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 52.92, 372.851, 290.578, 401.889 ],
    "font" : "TimesNewRoman",
    "font size" : 10.036,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "N2 are assumed to be ideal relays with outputs ±M01and ±M02 . Moreover, V1and V2 are the excitation signals."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 35,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 51.06, 344.649, 290.608, 370.764 ],
    "font" : "TimesNewRoman",
    "font size" : 10.036,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Let TY /R (s) be the input-output relation from the input R(s) to the outputY(s) , which is clearly derived as"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 36,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 51.06, 281.109, 291.521, 332.953 ],
    "font" : "TimesNewRoman",
    "font size" : 10.036,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "TY/R(s) = [I + P(s)G(s)]−1 P(s)G(s)F(s ) (8) where, G = G3G2N fG1 ( N f = diag (nf1,nf 2) , are the describing functions of the relays)."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 37,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 51.06, 181.464, 290.635, 281.041 ],
    "font" : "TimesNewRoman",
    "font size" : 10.036,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Due to uncertainty, P ∈{P} is a set of possible plants and it is assumed here that the plant set is finite or can be adequately approximated by a finite set so that numerical algorithms can be developed. The combined QFT/EEAS control design task is to find Gi(s) and F(s) with proper rational and stable elements, in order to satisfy the performance specifications ∀P ∈{P}. For example, tracking specifications may require that ∀P ∈{P},"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
